f(x)={e}^{x}$\n- $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$\n- $h(x)=x|x|$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|$ is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=x|x|$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fca40be-9c36-5fb8-ad26-209dd2633b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256931} {:answer/id #uuid "68981dbb-d346-555d-9b2c-1dc58df9f096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257709} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9af935-01b9-5d7d-8799-3657e9260c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258140} {:answer/id #uuid "b52e0ea7-f995-5d30-80b7-62df8855dd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258355} {:answer/id #uuid "d64c4c64-f1d1-5b63-97a1-2f295e43ae7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d64c4c64-f1d1-5b63-97a1-2f295e43ae7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258662} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b44df23-1d3f-536a-87e5-b4291d4bd2e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4fa44014-b4ce-590d-a5cb-5c0b5e464aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-125392" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dca985dc352c45477dbb6ada86ad5a9cfa4404969510191a1955e59b082709fe.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/eb/eb4da8143700c7d31e0ceacd09ac76a8d1bcb10379a34dce5b59d48f2ae58c13.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/70a473397216589350f63482ab3e36cb1247e7825046c70642254c8514564b00.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0331f7e8-0e35-5164-b8d1-5d386e06ec93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256807} {:answer/id #uuid "1730fa70-3c25-58c2-a16a-6801fc2014e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256988} {:answer/id #uuid "315ce396-c22b-59cd-bd06-2812540c5f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257217} {:answer/id #uuid "b0090bc6-7407-57ec-af44-9a91327f64d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258316} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb69fa4-d3b0-5b64-8e2f-5bb4c5df5146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0090bc6-7407-57ec-af44-9a91327f64d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258316} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a37d1db-a3f8-56ca-b8df-b3784b209996" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a58b62d-04d0-5a47-b1b2-408fc180c352" :question/math-academy-id "q-125487" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9da7dc42ad4e9eb5b6df7c42f06d7a0f7767820693f8e99e073c7b8bd7e93441.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/14/14e94a2c5d0faabff1beeadeccb6999aa2edd71fce06fff8a2655db74e585eba.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c54487081f2150216b7e41b1351f0f7b19c794b140297789f19d9afe830bd7b1.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15483b1e-61e7-578f-bf79-f5cb406d02f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256973} {:answer/id #uuid "32f97d6f-86fb-531c-8adb-7d6039727dc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257231} {:answer/id #uuid "488a8740-b1a9-58ed-989d-8785e40268ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257418} {:answer/id #uuid "74fea565-2e34-55de-8443-d4e195fb8a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257807} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f1ff81-061c-5b97-bdb8-4996eded2ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "488a8740-b1a9-58ed-989d-8785e40268ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257418} :answer-field/id #uuid "d662c539-2d7d-5553-b1e9-d3fd8de37dbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35e675be-a5b1-5d4c-9024-2339a508b912" :question/math-academy-id "q-125491" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0daaf51b1aab2